---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Know Your Overtime Rights</h1>
  <p>Federal and state laws give most workers strong overtime protections. Here's what you should know.</p>

  <h2>You probably are entitled to overtime</h2>
  <p>The default under the FLSA is that all employees get overtime. To not pay overtime, your employer must show you fall into a specific exemption (executive, administrative, professional, outside sales, computer professional, etc.) AND meet both the salary basis and duties tests for that exemption.</p>
  <p>Being salaried doesn't automatically make you exempt. Being called a "manager" doesn't either. The duties you actually perform matter.</p>

  <h2>Your employer must keep records</h2>
  <p>FLSA requires employers to keep accurate records of hours worked for non-exempt employees. If your hours aren't being recorded properly, that's a problem &mdash; for you and for them.</p>

  <h2>Off-the-clock work counts</h2>
  <p>Work performed before clocking in, after clocking out, during unpaid meal breaks, or remotely (checking email, taking calls) generally counts toward your hours. Employers can't avoid overtime by having you do work "off the clock."</p>

  <h2>Retaliation is illegal</h2>
  <p>Both federal and most state laws prohibit retaliation against employees who complain about wage violations or file claims. Firing, demoting, or punishing an employee for asserting overtime rights is itself a separate legal violation.</p>

  <h2>You can recover back pay</h2>
  <p>If your employer underpaid overtime, you can recover the unpaid wages plus an equal amount as "liquidated damages" under federal law (so double the back wages). The statute of limitations is generally 2 years (3 if the violation was willful).</p>

  <h2>How to file a wage claim</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Document your hours.</strong> Keep your own records: dates, times worked, what you did. Texts, emails, schedules &mdash; save anything that proves your hours.</li>
    <li><strong>Calculate what you're owed.</strong> Use our <a href="/">overtime calculator</a> for an estimate.</li>
    <li><strong>Try internal resolution first if it's safe.</strong> Sometimes payroll errors are unintentional. A polite email to HR with documentation can resolve the issue.</li>
    <li><strong>If that fails, file with the US Department of Labor's Wage and Hour Division</strong> (dol.gov/whd) or your state's labor agency. Filing is free.</li>
    <li><strong>Consider an attorney</strong> for larger amounts. Many wage-and-hour attorneys work on contingency.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>For practical guidance, see <a href="/guides/what-to-do-if-your-employer-wont-pay-overtime/">What to do if your employer won't pay overtime</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
